      "number": 6,
      "content": "🌸Chapter 6\nThe Girl Behind the Words\n\nOpening Quote\n\n\"Sometimes we hide behind ink...\n\nbecause we're afraid our voices won't be enough.\"\n\nThe first letter should have been the last.\n\nYoon-ah knew that.\n\nShe repeated it to herself the entire weekend.\n\nDon't write another one.\n\nYou'll only make things complicated.\n\nYou'll regret it.\n\nBut every time she sat at her desk, her pen found the paper before her thoughts could stop it.\n\nMonday arrived with bright sunshine.\n\nThe rain was gone, leaving the campus washed clean.\n\nStudents hurried across the courtyard, laughing as if the world had never been heavy.\n\nYoon-ah walked through the gates with a folded piece of paper tucked inside her literature textbook.\n\nShe could feel it with every step.\n\nLike it had its own heartbeat.\n\nThe library doors opened with their familiar creak.\n\nShe looked toward the window.\n\nHis seat was empty.\n\nFor some reason, she felt relieved.\n\nShe still had time.\n\nMoving as naturally as she could, she walked to the shelf where he always borrowed the same novel.\n\nHer fingers trembled as she opened it.\n\nThe first letter was gone.\n\nA smile she couldn't control appeared on her face.\n\n\nHe had taken it.\n\nHe hadn't thrown it away.\n\nHe had kept reading.\n\nSlowly, she slipped the second letter between the pages.\n\nThis one was shorter.\n\n\"Today the sun came out after three days of rain.\n\nI wondered if people notice sunshine the same way they notice kindness.\n\nOnly after they've lived without it.\"\n\nShe closed the book gently and returned it to the shelf.\n\nJust in time.\n\n\"Morning.\"\n\nHis voice came from behind her.\n\nYoon-ah nearly dropped the book.\n\nShe turned around.\n\nJae-hyun stood there, holding two cups of coffee.\n\n\"I didn't mean to scare you.\"\n\n\"I'm... okay.\"\n\nHe noticed the book in her hands.\n\n\"Oh, you're reading this one too?\"\n\nShe looked at the cover, suddenly aware of how suspicious she must seem.\n\n\"I... I like novels.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\nFor a brief second, they stood facing each other, both holding the same book.\n\nIt felt strangely intimate.\n\nAs though they shared a secret.\n\nOnly one of them actually did.\n\n\n\"I'll see you inside,\" he said before walking toward their usual table.\n\nYoon-ah watched him go.\n\nThen quietly whispered,\n\n\"You already will.\"\n\nThat afternoon, he opened the book.\n\nThe second letter slipped onto the desk.\n\nHe picked it up.\n\nRead it once.\n\nThen read it again.\n\nA small smile touched his lips.\n\n\"Hmm...\"\n\nHe folded it carefully instead of putting it back.\n\nYoon-ah pretended to highlight a paragraph.\n\nIn reality, she couldn't read a single word.\n\nShe watched his reflection in the window.\n\nHe was smiling at something she had written.\n\nIt should have made her happy.\n\nInstead, it made her heart ache.\n\nHe loved the words.\n\nBut he didn't know the person behind them.\n\nThe next day, something unexpected happened.\n\nWhen Jae-hyun arrived, he didn't go straight to his seat.\n\nInstead, he walked to the librarian.\n\nYoon-ah couldn't hear their conversation.\n\nBut she saw him holding up one of the letters.\n\nThe librarian shook her head.\n\nHe thanked her politely and returned to his table.\n\n\nHe looked... thoughtful.\n\nAs if he had hoped for an answer.\n\nLater that afternoon, he murmured to himself while staring at the folded paper.\n\n\"I wonder who you are.\"\n\nYoon-ah's fingers stopped moving.\n\n\"I wonder...\" he continued softly, \"...if you've been trying to tell someone something.\"\n\nHis eyes rested on the letter for a long moment.\n\nThen he smiled sadly.\n\n\"I hope they listen.\"\n\nYoon-ah lowered her head.\n\nIf only he knew.\n\nThe person she was trying to tell...\n\nwas sitting right there.\n\nOnly a few feet away.\n\nYet somehow, farther than anyone else in the room.\n\nThat evening, she wrote again.\n\n\"You wondered who I am today.\n\nFor one second, I wanted to tell you.\n\nBut I realized something...\n\nIt's easier to love you from these pages than from across the table.\n\nOn paper, I never have to see the disappointment in your eyes.\"\n\nShe folded the letter carefully.\n\nThen closed her notebook.\n\nFor the first time since this began, she whispered the truth aloud.\n\n\"I think I'm falling in love.\"\n\nThe room was silent.\n\nOnly the moon looked in through her window.\n\n\nAnd somehow...\n\nit felt like the only witness she could trust.\n\nEnding Quote\n\n\"He knew the handwriting.\n\nHe just didn't know the heart.\""
